  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The parabola's vertex lies on the symmetry axis, so we will start by computing the axis of symmetry. \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=1$ and $b=-6$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{(-6)}{2(1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-6)}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=3.$ This also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we substitute $x=3$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{2}-6x+7 \\\\  & ={3}^{2}-6(3)+7 \\\\  & =9-18+7 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(3,-2).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
